Report a game that stopped for good, instead of freezing silently

When the emulated CPU takes an exception (a jump to a bad address,
Core_ExecException; a bad memory access, Core_MemoryException; a broken
savestate's AntiCrashCallback), Core_Break puts the core in CORE_STEPPING_CPU,
and a runtime error leaves it in CORE_RUNTIME_ERROR. The libretro port has no
debugger to step it out again: its run loop's default case "is not handling
the various states used for debugging", so every later retro_run returns
without emulating a cycle. The player sees the last picture frozen and hears
nothing, with no way to tell a crash from a hang.

After the frame, these three states now call RETRO_ENVIRONMENT_SHUTDOWN once
per boot (cleared when a game loads and on reset), the call the port already
makes when a boot fails, so the frontend can stop the game and say so.

Nothing in normal play reaches them: the save, load and rewind paths call
Core_Break only from CORE_RUNTIME_ERROR (SaveState.cpp), the libretro port
serializes through SaveState::SaveStart without that path, and the other
callers are debugger, breakpoint and RAIntegration (Windows) features this
port does not run.

diff --git a/libretro/libretro.cpp b/libretro/libretro.cpp
index 3b2f103..c95d60e 100644
--- a/libretro/libretro.cpp
+++ b/libretro/libretro.cpp
@@ -109,6 +109,8 @@ namespace Libretro
 
    bool g_pendingBoot = false;
    std::string g_bootErrorString;
+   // A halt (see retro_run) is reported once per boot.
+   static bool g_haltReported = false;
 
    static bool detectVsyncSwapInterval = false;
    static bool detectVsyncSwapIntervalOptShown = true;
@@ -1378,6 +1380,22 @@ namespace Libretro
          // Reached the end of the frame while running at full blast, all good. Set back to running for the next frame
          coreState = CORE_RUNNING_CPU;
          break;
+      case CORE_STEPPING_CPU:
+      case CORE_STEPPING_GE:
+      case CORE_RUNTIME_ERROR:
+         // The game has stopped for good. With no debugger in this port, the only
+         // ways here are an emulated CPU exception (Core_ExecException,
+         // Core_MemoryException, a broken savestate's AntiCrashCallback) or a
+         // runtime error, and nothing will ever step the core out of them: every
+         // later retro_run would return without emulating a cycle, a frozen
+         // picture and silence. Tell the frontend, once, the way a failed boot
+         // does, so it can say so. (Retro Pal, 2026-10-27.)
+         if (!g_haltReported) {
+            g_haltReported = true;
+            ERROR_LOG(Log::CPU, "Core halted in state %s", CoreStateToString(coreState));
+            environ_cb(RETRO_ENVIRONMENT_SHUTDOWN, nullptr);
+         }
+         break;
       default:
          // We're not handling the various states used for debugging in the libretro port.
          break;
@@ -1547,6 +1565,7 @@ bool retro_load_game(const struct retro_game_info *game)
    coreParam.cpuCore         =  (CPUCore)g_Config.iCpuCore;
 
    g_pendingBoot = true;
+   g_haltReported = false;
 
    struct retro_core_option_display option_display;
 
@@ -1596,6 +1615,7 @@ void retro_unload_game(void)
 void retro_reset(void)
 {
    PSP_Shutdown(true);
+   g_haltReported = false;
 
    if (BootState::Complete != PSP_Init(PSP_CoreParameter(), &g_bootErrorString))
    {
